"finite-certificates/README.md at main · reuellee/finite-certificates · GitHub (https://ithub.global.ssl.fastly.net/reuellee/finite-certificates/blob/main/README.md)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; `attack_maxout66.md` + `verify_maxout66_d4n4.py` (+ `cert_d4n4.json`): explicit 32-vertex exact-rational certificate confirming achievability in the smallest open case (d,n)=(4,4) of Conjecture 6.6(2) of Balakin–Cox–Loho–Sturmfels, Maxout Polytopes (arXiv:2509.21286), where the paper had no computational evidence for d ≥ 4. `target_list.md` is the ranked harvest of further attackable ML-theory conjectures.\n### ai/maxout/ — smallest open case of a 2025 Sturmfels-et-al conjecture resolved; (4,6) resolved and (3,8) achievability confirmed; odd-n resistance certified\n\n`attack_maxout66.md` + `verify_maxout66_d4n4.py` (+ `cert_d4n4.json`): explicit 32-vertex exact-rational certificate confirming achievability in the smallest open case (d,n)=(4,4) of Conjecture 6.6(2) of Balakin–Cox–Loho–Sturmfels, Maxout Polytopes (arXiv:2509.21286), where the paper had no computational evidence for d ≥ 4. `target_list.md` is the ranked harvest of further attackable ML-theory conjectures.\n\nSecond attack (2026-07-30) — `attack_c66_deficit.md` + `verify_c66_new_cases.py` (5 certificates, ~2 s, stdlib Fractions): (4,6) = 104 confirmed (part 2's first n > d case; the conjectured maximum is the cap, so the case closes) and (3,8) = 110 confirmed (achievability of the even-n formula at the first n beyond the paper's DFS range). At the odd-n cases the conjectured maxima were not reproduced despite the paper's own sampling recipe at 15× its budget, ~300 complete-per-direction-set branch-and-bound searches, and generator-drop seeding from exact even-n extremals: certified exact counts 42/58/84 at (3,5)/(4,5)/(3,7) vs conjectured 44/60/88. As of that note these were lower bounds only and nothing was refuted; an out-of-sample-falsified intermediate \"deficit law\" is documented there with full honesty scoping.\n\nCapstone (2026-07-31) — `capstone/CAPSTONE.md`: THEOREM, max f₀(3,5) = 42, so the (3,5) row above is now settled in both directions and the odd case of Conjecture 6.6.1 is refuted at n = 5, as is the tightness of Prop 6.5 there. The upper bound is 132,560 exact cell-wide Gordan certificates plus a symmetry reduction to one oriented-matroid cell; the primary re-verification (`capstone/independent_audit.py`, stdlib, ~2 min) imports nothing from the programs that generated the library. A submission-ready note is in `ai/maxout/paper/`. (4,5) and (3,7) remain lower bounds — 58 and 84 are attained; whether their true maxima fall below 60 and 88 is open.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2509.21286] Maxout Polytopes (https://accelresearching.com/accel-zinc-picolinate-50mg/?_=%2Fabs%2F2509.21286%23nLw79nwZb1b3uPkZUsgpm5M%3D)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Title:Maxout Polytopes ... Cite as:  | arXiv:2509.21286 [math.CO]\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 25 Sep 2025]\n\n# Title:Maxout Polytopes\n\nAuthors:Andrei Balakin, Shelby Cox, Georg Loho, Bernd Sturmfels\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Maxout Polytopes, by Andrei Balakin and 3 other authors\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:Maxout polytopes are defined by feedforward neural networks with maxout activation function and non-negative weights after the first layer. We characterize the parameter spaces and extremal f-vectors of maxout polytopes for shallow networks, and we study the separating hypersurfaces which arise when a layer is added to the network. We also show that maxout polytopes are cubical for generic networks without bottlenecks.\n\nComments:  | 24 pages, 3 figures\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO); Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2509.21286 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2509.21286v1 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.21286 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Andrei Balakin [view email]\n[v1] Thu, 25 Sep 2025 15:06:10 UTC (1,911 KB)\n\nFull-text links:\n\n## Access Paper:\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Maxout Polytopes, by Andrei Balakin and 3 other authors\n\n  * View PDF\n  * HTML (experimental)\n  * TeX Source--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA mirror deformation of Markov numbers - arXiv (https://arxiv-troller.com/paper/3091732/)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; 2602.14802 ... We introduce a deformed squared Markov equation given by $X^2 + Y^2 + Z^2 + (q+q^{-1})(XY+YZ+XZ) = 3(1 + q + q^{-1})XYZ$.\n\n# A mirror deformation of Markov numbers\n\nAuthors: LÃ©a Bittmann, Perrine Jouteur, Ezgi KantarcÄ± OÄuz, Melody Molander, Emine YÄ±ldÄ±rÄ±m\n\narXiv ID\n\n2602.14802\n\nSubmitted\n\nFebruary 16, 2026\n\nLast Updated\n\nFebruary 17, 2026\n\nCategories\n\nmath.CO\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe introduce a deformed squared Markov equation given by $X^2 + Y^2 + Z^2 + (q+q^{-1})(XY+YZ+XZ) = 3(1 + q + q^{-1})XYZ$. Symmetric solutions of this new equation present a remarkable factorization property which allows us to talk about their square roots. These square roots give a natural $q$-deformation of the Markov numbers that has not previously occurred in the literature. We call them mirror Markov numbers. We prove a characterization of mirror Markov numbers and discover a mutation rule, mirror mutation, to generate them all. We also prove a geometric realization of the corresponding mirror mutation on a once-punctured sphere with three orbifold points. Our mirror deformation leads to deformations of Fibonacci and Pell branches for which we give precise formulas. Furthermore, the deformed squared Markov equation specializes to many other very well known generalized Markov equations. We also obtain the super Markov numbers from a specialization of the deformed squared Markov numbers, which we use to prove a conjecture of Musiker.\n\nView on arXiv ↗ Find Similar Papers--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - reuellee/finite-certificates: Small, explicit, machine-checkable research results in exact arithmetic — SAE interpretability metrics and optimizer theory. Every claim ships a standalone verifier. · GitHub (https://github.com/reuellee/finite-certificates)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; This refutes tightness of Proposition 6.5 and the odd `n=5` case of Conjecture 6.6.1 in Balakin–Cox–Loho–Sturmfels, Maxout Polytopes (arXiv:2509.21286).\n# finite-certificates\n\nA repository of small, explicit, machine-checkable research results. The guiding rule is simple: search can be heuristic; claims must be exact. Most theorem-level claims ship with standalone verifiers using integers, rational arithmetic, or exact symbolic computation, plus an adversarial review trail.\n\nThe repository currently has three major research fronts: exact discrete geometry, oriented-matroid realizability/topology, and machine-learning theory counterexamples.\n\n## Current headline results\n\n### 1. Maxout polytopes: `max f₀(3,5) = 42`\n\nThe maximum number of vertices of a 3-dimensional zonoboxtope with 5 generators is exactly 42. This refutes tightness of Proposition 6.5 and the odd `n=5` case of Conjecture 6.6.1 in Balakin–Cox–Loho–Sturmfels, Maxout Polytopes (arXiv:2509.21286).\n\nThe upper bound is backed by 132,560 exact cell-wide certificates, with an independent standard-library audit and an arXiv-ready note.\n\n  * `ai/maxout/`\n  * `ai/maxout/paper/`\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA mirror deformation of Markov numbers | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2602.14802v1)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: last month; The classical Markov equation $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 3xyz$x 2+y 2+z 2=3 x y z has been a fundamental object in number theory since the 19th century, with its positive integer solutions forming the famous Markov tree through \"Vieta jumping\" operations. ... In this geometric model, each cluster variable is replaced by a pair of \"tagged\" arcs, and the mirror mutation rule corresponds to specific geometric exchange relations. ... @misc{bittmann2026mirrordeformationmarkovnumbers, title={A mirror deformation of Markov numbers}, author={Léa Bittmann and Perrine Jouteur and Ezgi Kantarcı Oğuz and Melody Molander and Emine Yıldırım}, year={2026}, eprint={2602.14802}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14802}, }\nSubmitted 16 Feb 2026\n\nen\n\n# A mirror deformation of Markov numbers\n\nLB\n\nLéa Bittmann\n\nPJ\n\nPerrine Jouteur\n\nEK\n\nEzgi Kantarcı Oğuz\n\nMM\n\nMelody Molander\n\nEY\n\nEmine Yıldırım\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe introduce a deformed squared Markov equation given by $X^2 + Y^2 + Z^2 + (q+q^{-1})(XY+YZ+XZ) = 3(1 + q + q^{-1})XYZ$X 2+Y 2+Z 2+(q+q−1)(X Y+Y Z+X Z)=3(1+q+q−1)X Y Z. Symmetric solutions of this new equation present a remarkable factorization property which allows us to talk about their square roots. These square roots give a natural $q$q-deformation of the Markov numbers that has not previously occurred in the literature. We call them mirror Markov numbers. We prove a characterization of mirror Markov numbers and discover a mutation rule, mirror mutation, to generate them all. We also prove a geometric realization of the corresponding mirror mutation on a once-punctured sphere with three orbifold points. Our mirror deformation leads to deformations of Fibonacci and Pell branches for which we give precise formulas. \nThis computational approach not only provides rigorous proofs but also suggests algorithmic methods for generating and studying these new q-deformed sequences. The continued fraction representation offers an alternative perspective on the combinatorial structure underlying the mirror Markov numbers.\n\n## Open Questions and Future Directions\n\nThe paper concludes with several compelling conjectures that outline future research directions. The general positivity conjecture for all mirror Markov numbers remains open, despite being verified computationally for many cases. Additionally, a q-deformed analog of the classical uniqueness conjecture for Markov numbers is proposed.\n\nThe authors also conjecture the existence of a generalized q-Markov function that remains constant along various branches of the mirror Markov tree, generalizing known results for classical and k-generalized Markov numbers. These open problems provide clear targets for future investigation and suggest that the mirror deformation contains rich mathematical structure yet to be fully understood.\n\nThe work represents a significant advance in the study of Markov numbers, successfully bridging diverse areas including cluster algebras, super-Teichmüller theory, and q-deformed combinatorics while opening new avenues for exploration in each of these fields.\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{bittmann2026mirrordeformationmarkovnumbers, title={A mirror deformation of Markov numbers}, author={Léa Bittmann and Perrine Jouteur and Ezgi Kantarcı Oğuz and Melody Molander and Emine Yıldırım}, year={2026}, eprint={2602.14802}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14802}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKatalog Plus der Bibliothek der Frankfurt UAS: Maxout Polytopes (https://hds.hebis.de/fuas/EBSCO/Record?id=edsarx.2509.21286%7Cedsarx)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; # Maxout Polytopes ... DOI:  | 10.48550/arXiv.2509.21286\n\n# Maxout Polytopes\n\nTitle:  | Maxout Polytopes\nAuthors:  | Balakin, Andrei; Cox, Shelby; Loho, Georg; Sturmfels, Bernd\nPublication Year:  | 2025\nSubject Terms:  | Combinatoric